  select $l27_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is the classic 'habitable zone,' and why does the chapter call it a useful first cut but a dangerous oversimplification?", "options": ["The region inside a star where planets can't form; it's fine as-is", "The band of distances from a star where starlight can keep SURFACE water liquid \u2014 useful for flagging Earth-like candidates, but it wrongly assumes warmth must come from the star and water must sit on the surface, so it misses tidally-heated subsurface oceans far outside it (Europa, Enceladus)", "The zone where only intelligent life can evolve", "A fixed band at 1 AU around every star"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The habitable zone is the 'Goldilocks' band where a planet is neither so hot that surface water boils (Venus) nor so cold that it freezes (Mars). It's the organizing principle of the exoplanet search. But it answers only one question \u2014 can starlight keep surface water liquid? \u2014 while life needs only liquid water and energy, neither of which must come from a star. Tidal heating keeps subsurface oceans liquid on the frozen moons of the giant planets, hundreds of millions of miles beyond any zone. Clinging to the zone would make us look right past some of the most promising abodes for life."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How does the habitable zone's location depend on the star, and why?", "options": ["It's always at 1 AU regardless of the star", "It scales as roughly the square root of the star's luminosity \u2014 farther out for a bright star, closer in for a dim one \u2014 because a planet's received starlight follows the inverse-square law, so it must sit at the distance that gives a moderate amount of warmth", "It's closer for bright stars and farther for dim ones", "It depends only on the planet's size, not the star"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A brighter star pours out more energy, so a planet must be farther away to receive a moderate (not scorch